# Test: `ze send bgp <selector> raw hex <data>` puts the operator's octets on
# one peer's wire, and every selector that does not name exactly one session is
# refused by name.
#
# The .ci cannot drive this alone. The daemon publishes its ephemeral SSH
# address into the file ZE_SSH_EPHEMERAL names, and only a compiled fixture can
# read that file and put ZE_SSH_HOST and ZE_SSH_PORT on the client. So the
# fixture starts both halves, a daemon over ephemeral SSH and the ze-test peer
# this file scripts, and drives `ze send` as argv against them.
#
# The peer holds the assertion. It exits zero only when both expectations below
# arrived in order, so the run proves the injected octets reached the wire
# rather than only that the command exited zero.
#
# The second expectation is the whole injected message, marker and header
# included, because the command types no type keyword. It withdraws
# 10.9.9.0/24, which is what makes it distinguishable from the End-of-RIB
# marker the session already sent.
#
# VALIDATES: `ze send bgp 127.0.0.1 raw hex FFFF...` reaches handleRaw,
#   ResolveSinglePeer and SendRawMessage, and the peer reads the octets
#   unchanged; a wildcard selector and an exclusion selector are each refused
#   by name and send nothing; and the path this grammar left, `peer <selector>
#   raw`, matches nothing.
# PREVENTS: the single-session guard going unproven at the path an operator now
#   types, where the selector sits two tokens further from the eye than it did,
#   and unvalidated bytes would reach every configured session.

tmpfs=peer-script:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001B020004180A09090000
EOF_PEER

option=timeout:value=180s

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture ui/send-raw-reaches-one-peer $PORT:timeout=170s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK
